    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d862">

                <pb facs="1143" n="1133" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1133"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.461/2–1646: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-02-16T16:00:00+07:00">February 16,
                            1946—4 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 17—5:39 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>314. Reopening of consular offices Manchuria (Dept’s 283, Feb 13<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d862-f1"/> and Embassy’s 193, Jan [30]). Vice Minister Foreign
                    Affairs Liu Chieh informed me today that (sent Dept 314, repeated Shanghai 104),
                    while Foreign Office would like to have our consular officers return to
                    Manchuria, under present conditions in Manchuria, it would not be practicable
                    for the time being.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
